{"status": "ok", "message-type": "work-list", "message-version": "1.0.0", "message": {"facets": {}, "total-results": 32835, "items": [{"abstract": "<jats:p>The psychological principles that govern the perception of decision problems and the evaluation of probabilities and outcomes produce predictable shifts of preference when the same problem is framed in different ways. Reversals of preference are demonstrated in choices regarding monetary outcomes, both hypothetical and real, and in questions pertaining to the loss of human lives. The effects of frames on preferences are compared to the effects of perspectives on perceptual appearance. The dependence of preferences on the formulation of decision problems is a significant concern for the theory of rational choice.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1126/science.7455683", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2006, 10, 5]], "date-time": "2006-10-05T13:45:58Z", "timestamp": 1160055958000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 12339, "title": ["The Framing of Decisions and the Psychology of Choice"], "author": [{"given": "Amos", "family": "Tversky", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Daniel", "family": "Kahneman", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Science"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[1981, 1, 30]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[1981, 1, 30]]}}, {"DOI": "10.1111/j.1460-2466.1993.tb01304.x", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2006, 2, 7]], "date-time": "2006-02-07T06:58:09Z", "timestamp": 1139295489000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 10929, "title": ["Framing: Toward Clarification of a Fractured Paradigm"], "author": [{"given": "Robert M.", "family": "Entman", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Journal of Communication"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[1993, 12, 1]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[1993, 12, 1]]}}, {"abstract": "<jats:p>The recent proliferation of scholarship on collective action frames and framing processes in relation to social movements indicates that framing processes have come to be regarded, alongside resource mobilization and political opportunity processes, as a central dynamic in understanding the character and course of social movements. This review examines the analytic utility of the framing literature for understanding social movement dynamics. We first review how collective action frames have been conceptualized, including their characteristic and variable features. We then examine the literature related to framing dynamics and processes. Next we review the literature regarding various contextual factors that constrain and facilitate framing processes. We conclude with an elaboration of the consequences of framing processes for other movement processes and outcomes. We seek throughout to provide clarification of the linkages between framing concepts/processes and other conceptual and theoretical formulations relevant to social movements, such as schemas and ideology.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1146/annurev.soc.26.1.611", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2002, 7, 27]], "date-time": "2002-07-27T07:48:40Z", "timestamp": 1027756120000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 6558, "title": ["Framing Processes and Social Movements: An Overview and Assessment"], "author": [{"given": "Robert D.", "family": "Benford", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Department of Sociology, University of Nebraska, Lincoln, Nebraska 68588-0324;"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "David A.", "family": "Snow", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Department of Sociology, University of Arizona, Tucson, Arizona 85721;"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Annual Review of Sociology"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2000, 8]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2000, 8]]}}, {"DOI": "10.1086/296365", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2002, 7, 26]], "date-time": "2002-07-26T13:14:48Z", "timestamp": 1027689288000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2814, "title": ["Rational Choice and the Framing of Decisions"], "author": [{"given": "Amos", "family": "Tversky", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Daniel", "family": "Kahneman", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["The Journal of Business"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[1986, 1]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[1986, 1]]}}, {"abstract": "<jats:p>▪ Abstract  We review the meaning of the concept of framing, approaches to studying framing, and the effects of framing on public opinion. After defining framing and framing effects, we articulate a method for identifying frames in communication and a psychological model for understanding how such frames affect public opinion. We also discuss the relationship between framing and priming, outline future research directions, and describe the normative implications of framing.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1146/annurev.polisci.10.072805.103054", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2007, 5, 4]], "date-time": "2007-05-04T19:08:57Z", "timestamp": 1178305737000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2775, "title": ["Framing Theory"], "author": [{"given": "Dennis", "family": "Chong", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Department of Political Science, Northwestern University, Evanston, Illinois 60208;"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "James N.", "family": "Druckman", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Department of Political Science, Northwestern University, Evanston, Illinois 60208;"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Annual Review